#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124202 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74f126da-5f3b-5af8-9aee-d6f0a4c61c49" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Shape of a Level Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "5791bd77-96c7-571d-b8b5-76cb62eb0a9d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4228" :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2+2y^2-2}{x^2}$ are given by $f(x,y) = c$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $c=1$ gives a circle\n2. $c=0$ gives an ellipse\n3. $c=2$ gives a hyperbola" :question/worked-solution "Setting\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = c\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2} + 2y^{2} - 2}{x^{2}} &= c \\\\\nx^{2} + 2y^{2} - 2 &= cx^{2} \\\\\n(1 - c)x^{2} + 2y^{2} &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c = 1$, then the level curve is $y^2 = 1$, which consists of two lines.\n- If $c = 0$, then the level curve is $x^2+2y^2=2$, which is an ellipse.\n- If $c = 2$, then the level curve is $2y^2-x^2=2$ which is a hyperbola.\n\nSo, only statements II and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124203 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfd8982d-d859-5eb2-9f35-69af39c04822" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Level Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "618e7097-38d3-5167-a9d2-5d5a3fc85e0a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4229" :question/problem "Sketch the level curves of the surface $z=e^{x^2-y^2}$." :question/worked-solution "The level curves for the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=e^{x^2-y^2}\n$$\n\ntake the form\n\n$$\ne^{x^2-y^2} = c\n$$\n\nNote that we must have the constraint $c\\gt 0$ since $e^x \\gt 0$ for all $x$.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\ne^{x^{2} - y^{2}} = c; \\ln (e^{x^{2} - y^{2}}) = \\ln c; x^{2} - y^{2} = \\ln c\n$$\n\nWe can split this into three cases, depending on the sign of $\\ln c$.\n\n- Case 1: $\\ln c \\gt 0$. In this case, we have the family of hyperbolas $x^2-y^2 = \\ln c$.\n- Case 2: $\\ln c = 0$. In this case, we have the lines $y=\\pm x$.\n- Case 3: $\\ln c < 0$. In this case, we have the family of hyperbolas $y^2 - x^2 = -\\ln c$.\n\nTherefore, the level curves are as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1900/Source/Images/d07a616a086987c45b478dda907c99c1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124280 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62dff73b-1857-5192-ae10-fe22577cbfb4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Level Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "fe87d9e4-6445-5ee7-9dc7-6ab8fbb165c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-5968" :question/problem "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $G\\big(\\sqrt{5}, 1\\big)$ for the function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{12y}{x^2-y^2}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $G$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f\\big(\\sqrt{5}, 1\\big)&= \\dfrac{12(1)}{\\big(\\sqrt{5}\\big)^2 - 1^2} = 3 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $G\\big(\\sqrt{5}, 1\\big)$ is the $3$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = c\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=3$. So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x, y) &= c \\\\\n\\frac{12y}{x^{2} - y^{2}} &= 3 \\\\\n4y &= x^{2} - y^{2} \\\\\nx^{2} - y^{2} - 4y - 4 &= -4 \\\\\nx^{2} - (y^{2} + 4y + 4) &= -4 \\\\\nx^{2} - (y + 2)^{2} &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $G\\big(\\sqrt{5}, 1\\big)$ is the hyperbola\n\n$$\nx^2 - (y+2)^2 = -4\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125095 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfd8982d-d859-5eb2-9f35-69af39c04822" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Level Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fbf0002c-c923-5954-a281-bec2561af96b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1900/Source/Images/q-64908-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123593} {:answer/id #uuid "1e9c41e6-b29e-555a-aaeb-bd0982dc19d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1900/Source/Images/q-64908-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123594} {:answer/id #uuid "39677a50-9f2e-50ba-a5f4-48174136c5f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1900/Source/Images/q-64908-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123595} {:answer/id #uuid "3db740ac-5f62-543d-8d00-61b4a77ba230" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1900/Source/Images/q-64908-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123596} {:answer/id #uuid "4618caf3-b1f6-5171-b9dc-94ba193a7ec7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1900/Source/Images/q-64908-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e9c41e6-b29e-555a-aaeb-bd0982dc19d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1900/Source/Images/q-64908-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123594} :answer-field/id #uuid "12eba2d0-685f-5666-bc00-d779156f5653" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123592}] :question/id #uuid "5348f454-8724-5136-a5f0-8c87b0d92c2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-64908" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the level curves of $f(x, y) = \\ln (y - 2x^{2})$?"}]}
